{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Dan, a lion's whelp.</b> He too was near the belt [of Philistia]; therefore his metaphor is of lions (there)." "<b>He shall leap from the Bashan.</b> As its Targum [renders] — that the Jordan emerges from his territory, from the cave of Paneas, and that is Leshem, which is in the territory of Dan, as it is said (Joshua 19) ״וַיִּקְרְאוּ לְלֶשֶׁם דָּן״, and its leaping and springing is from the Bashan. Another interpretation: just as this זִנּוּק emerges from one place and is divided to two places, so too the tribe of Dan took a portion in two places — first they took in the northwest, Ekron and its surroundings, and it did not suffice them, and they came and fought against Leshem, which is Paneas, and it is in the northeast — for the Jordan emerges from the cave of Paneas, and it is in the east of the land of Israel, and comes from north to south and ends at the edge of the Salt Sea, which is in the east of Judah, which [Judah] took in the south of the land of Israel — as is spelled out in the book of Joshua, and that is what is said (there) ״וַיֵּצֵא גְבוּל בְּנֵי דָן מֵהֶם וַיַּעֲלוּ בְנֵי דָן וַיִּלָּחֲמוּ עִם לֶשֶׁם וְגוֹ׳״ — their boundary went out from the entire direction in which they had begun to take their inheritance (Sifrei)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:49:52.304953774Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}